export const hvacCategories:Category[] = [
  group('best-vacuum-pumps-for-hvac','Best Vacuum Pumps for HVAC: Capacity, Oil Changes and Refrigerant Fit','Evacuation & Recovery','Compare VP87 for a DC-motor 8 CFM setup, VP67 for a smaller Fieldpiece option, VPX7 for higher nominal capacity, VP833 for its documented A3 role, and JB DV-200N for a conventional alternative.','These are documented-capability comparisons, not timed evacuation results.',[['Compare the whole evacuation path','The pump, hose diameter, connections, restrictions, oil condition and system volume work together. Nominal CFM is not a promised completion time.'],['Keep compatibility model-specific','A3 compatibility on VP833 does not apply to all Fieldpiece pumps. The exact refrigerant and current machine instructions decide suitability.'],['Budget for measurement and maintenance','Choose a suitable standalone vacuum gauge and the required hoses. Oil maintenance and an appropriate decay assessment are part of the equipment plan.']],[['Is a bigger pump always better?','No. A restrictive connection can limit the setup. Compare capacity after confirming the intended system, hoses and service access.'],['Does a vacuum pump recover refrigerant?','No. Recovery equipment and an appropriate recovery cylinder serve a different purpose.']], [j.evacuation,j.commission,j.van],pumps,[['Choose VP87 for the arrangement, not a speed promise','The DC motor and RunQuick oil-change arrangement are reasons to compare VP87 for recurring work. We have not timed it against these alternatives. System volume, port access, hose choice and oil condition prevent a nominal pump-capacity figure from describing a completed evacuation.'],['Know when a different model is required','VP833 is the application-specific A3 comparison. JB suffixes can change electrical and compatibility details. Match the complete model and the current documentation before committing to a job or a purchase.'],['Plan a complete setup','The pump-only price omits a suitable gauge, hoses, compatible fittings and maintenance supplies. Choose equipment around the actual work and required measurement method; do not buy a larger pump to compensate for an unsuitable setup.']]),
  group('best-refrigerant-recovery-machines-for-hvac','Best Refrigerant Recovery Machines for HVAC: Digital and Twin-Cylinder Options','Evacuation & Recovery','Compare Fieldpiece MR45, Appion G5Twin, NAVAC NRDD, Robinair RG6 and JB F6-DP by the approved refrigerants, interface and complete recovery setup.','No recovery speeds were independently measured. Amazon availability and manufacturer-approved seller status are separate checks.',[['Check the actual refrigerant','Confirm current manufacturer approval for the exact unit and refrigerant; similar suffixes can describe a different machine.'],['Include the container and weight monitoring','Recovery requires suitable cylinders, hoses and weight monitoring. A machine’s stop function does not replace cylinder limits and the required procedure.'],['Compare maintenance and support','Consider service parts, authorized distribution, local repair options and the work volume that justifies owning the equipment.']],[['Can I use a vacuum pump instead?','No. An evacuation pump is not a recovery machine.'],['Is an Amazon offer automatically an authorized seller?','No. Appion specifically states Amazon and eBay are not approved distributors. Confirm eligibility directly with the manufacturer.']], [j.recovery,j.commission,j.van],recovery,[['When MR45 fits the business','The digital interface and variable-speed DC motor make MR45 a useful comparison for recurring recovery work. The decision depends on approved refrigerants, available service and the complete setup rather than an unmeasured fastest-machine claim.'],['Choose the exact regional model','US power requirements and refrigerant compatibility need checking against the actual part number. NRDD, NRDDF and JB regional suffixes are not interchangeable offers.'],['Include everything around the machine','The machine is one business purchase. The correct cylinder, scale, hoses, filters, maintenance and required qualified-work procedure are separate parts of the equipment plan.']]),
  group('best-refrigerant-leak-detectors-for-hvac','Best Refrigerant Leak Detectors for HVAC: Infrared, Heated Diode and Gas Scope','Refrigeration & Diagnostics','Start with DR82 for an infrared pinpointing comparison, DR58 for heated diode, DRX3 for its broader documented gas scope, or the INFICON D-TEK instruments for a different sensing workflow.','Leak indications require appropriate confirmation. Refrigerant approval and hazardous-location suitability must stay specific to each model.',[['Choose by gas and environment','Match the actual refrigerant or tracer gas and permitted environment, not just the words leak detector.'],['Compare sensing and maintenance','Infrared and heated-diode instruments differ in sensor care and operating behavior. Read the specified checks, filters and replacement requirements.'],['Separate hunting from pinpointing','Stratus offers cloud hunting as well as pinpointing. That is a different workflow from choosing an instrument for a known small suspect area.']],[['Can DR82 replace a combustible-gas detector?','Do not assume that. Use only its documented gas applications and operating environment.'],['Should I pick only by advertised sensitivity?','No. Approved gas, sensor maintenance, operating method and the kind of investigation matter too.']], [j.leak,j.service,j.commission],detectors,[['Why DR82 is a useful first comparison','Its infrared sensing approach provides a clear alternative to DR58’s heated diode. We have not compared detection speed, false indications or sensitivity in field conditions; the choice is based on documented design and application.'],['Broader gas support changes the purchase','DRX3’s broader listed scope is specific to DRX3. A similar brand name or housing is not permission to use another detector in a combustible environment. Read the current manual for the gas and conditions involved.'],['Keep the original feedback in context','Owner reports can explain handling and maintenance preferences, but a successful anecdote does not establish that a detector will identify every leak. Match the source to the model and use the documented confirmation method.']]),
  group('best-micron-gauges-for-hvac','Best Micron Gauges for HVAC: Standalone and Connected Vacuum Measurement','Evacuation & Recovery','Compare MG44 for local and Job Link viewing, Testo 552i for a connected probe, and BluVac+ LTE, Pro or Micro for different dedicated-gauge configurations.','The measurement location and setup matter; an ultimate pump vacuum is not the same thing as the system’s verified condition.',[['Decide where readings will be viewed','A local screen works without app viewing. A screenless probe needs a compatible receiving device.'],['Choose the record workflow','Compare trend, hold-test and report functions in the exact version. Similar family names do not guarantee identical features.'],['Keep the gauge suitable and maintained','Follow cleaning and connection instructions, protect the sensor and record measurement location with the result.']],[['Does my pump need a separate micron gauge?','A suitable gauge measures the system’s vacuum. A pump’s advertised capability does not prove the system reached it.'],['Is Testo 552i a standalone-screen instrument?','No. Its readings need a compatible app or receiving instrument.']], [j.evacuation,j.commission],gauges,[['MG44 separates measurement from the pump','A local display and compatible connected viewing are reasons to compare MG44. The instrument supports a measurement workflow; it does not select the correct evacuation target or decide whether a system is ready.'],['Choose the receiving device deliberately','For 552i, include a supported phone or manifold in the plan. If app access is inconvenient, a local-display gauge may be a better practical fit.'],['Compare BluVac versions by the required record','LTE, Pro and Micro deserve separate assessments. A business needing detailed repeatable records may value Pro functions; a smaller instrument may be appropriate for simpler work.']]),
  group('best-refrigerant-scales-for-hvac','Best Refrigerant Scales for HVAC: Local Displays and Connected Weighing','Refrigeration & Diagnostics','Choose SR47 for a Job Link weighing workflow, SRS1 for a simpler smaller-capacity scale, Testo 560i or NAVAC NRS2A for their connected systems, or CPS CC220 for a corded handheld readout.','Displayed weight and automatic valve control are different capabilities. Check every scale-only and valve-inclusive package separately.',[['Match capacity and resolution','Consider the complete cylinder weight, the required charge measurement and the published resolution.'],['Choose a reliable viewing arrangement','Decide between a corded handheld, wireless display and app integration according to actual access on the job.'],['Confirm automation components','A connected scale alone is not an automatic charging kit. Compatible valves, receiving devices and instructions are additional requirements.']],[['Does a scale prevent recovery-cylinder overfill?','No. Weight monitoring supports the required cylinder limits and procedure; it does not replace them.'],['Does a Testo 560i offer always include a valve?','No. Confirm the exact scale and valve package in the listing.']], [j.recovery,j.commission,j.service],scales,[['SR47 fits a connected measurement plan','Its useful distinction is organizing weight measurements with compatible instruments. That can improve documentation convenience without changing the requirement to choose and monitor the cylinder correctly.'],['Buy the complete configuration','An automatic charging workflow requires more than the platform. Compare the actual valve, receiving devices, supported functions and package number before comparing prices.'],['Simpler scales remain valid alternatives','A locally viewed SRS1 or corded CC220 can be appropriate when its capacity and resolution cover the work. Connectivity is useful only when it improves the workflow the technician actually uses.']]),
  group('best-clamp-meters-for-hvac','Best Clamp Meters for HVAC: Compact, Inrush and Connected Functions','Electrical & Airside','Compare SC680 for its broad AC/DC-current and connected functions, SC480 for fixed-head connected measurements, SC260 for compact carrying, SC440 for dual display and inrush, or SC640 for swivel access.','These five Fieldpiece models have different capabilities. We have not tested accuracy, head clearance or durability.',[['List the actual measurements','Separate AC jaw current, DC jaw current, inrush, capacitance and other functions. A meter reading DC voltage does not necessarily measure DC jaw current.'],['Check category and access','The complete meter-and-lead arrangement must meet the work environment. Head size and swivel access matter after rating and function requirements.'],['Do not buy for feature count','A larger connected instrument is useful when those functions are recurring. A compact tool can be sufficient only when it meets the same job requirements.']],[['Are SC480 and SC680 the same meter?','No. Compare current type, head design and documented functions separately.'],['Does owning a clamp authorize electrical work?','No. Use the required training, controls, procedure and suitable instrument for the specific measurement.']], [j.service,j.commission,j.apprentice],clamps,[['Why start the comparison with SC680','DC jaw-current measurement and a swivel head distinguish SC680 from narrower models. Those features are worthwhile when the systems and access conditions call for them, not because the broadest specification is always best.'],['Read smaller models on their own terms','SC260, SC440 and SC640 cover different carrying and measurement choices. Use the actual documented inrush and current capabilities rather than treating similar model names as interchangeable.'],['Keep measurement and authorization separate','Choose the instrument and leads for the electrical environment and follow the required qualified-work process. A clamp meter is not a universal substitute for isolation verification or another instrument required by the job.']]),
  group('best-manometers-for-hvac','Best Manometers for HVAC: Static Pressure, Differential and Probe Kits','Electrical & Airside','Compare SDMN6 when its pressure-switch tester is useful, SDMN5 for local differential pressure, JL3KM2 for a paired connected kit, JL3MN for one additional probe, or Testo 510i for a different app workflow.','Pressure measurements are not a complete furnace safety or combustion assessment.',[['Choose one or two measurement points','A single-port probe is an expansion component. A differential kit and its correct tubing/probes cover a different setup.'],['Separate pressure from combustion','A manometer measures pressure. Combustion analysis, CO assessment and appliance safety checks require their own appropriate instruments and procedures.'],['Confirm range and resolution','Gas and static-pressure tasks may need different ranges and fine resolution. Check the actual mode and approved test application.']],[['Does JL3MN equal the JL3KM2 kit?','No. JL3MN is a single probe; JL3KM2 is the documented paired kit.'],['Can a manometer replace a combustion analyzer?','No. They measure different things.']], [j.airflow,j.service,j.commission],manometers,[['SDMN6 adds a specific testing role','Compare it when its pressure-switch testing functions are part of trained recurring work. If the requirement is only static or differential pressure, a simpler instrument may be a better purchase.'],['Local and connected arrangements differ','A paired connected kit can support remote readings. A local dual-port instrument keeps the result at the tool. Consider placement and documentation before choosing the viewing method.'],['Pressure needs context','Keep the test location, mode and appliance or duct requirements with the reading. Neither a pressure result nor an app calculation independently establishes safe combustion or total delivered airflow.']]),
  group('best-psychrometers-for-hvac','Best Psychrometers for HVAC: Air Temperature and Humidity','Electrical & Airside','Compare JL3RH and Testo 605i for connected air-condition readings, or the newer Testo 625 for a local display. These three cover distinct viewing and placement choices without padding the list.','Temperature, humidity and derived wet-bulb values are different from a direct airflow measurement.',[['Match measurement location','Room conditions and in-duct conditions can differ. Choose a probe that can be placed at the required location.'],['Plan paired measurements','A single probe may not cover simultaneous supply and return measurements. Check kit contents and the planned receiving device.'],['Keep derived readings in context','Document dry bulb, humidity and measurement locations. A temperature split alone does not diagnose every fault.']],[['Is a psychrometer an airflow meter?','No. Air conditions and air velocity are separate measurements.'],['Are all Testo 625 listings the same?','No. The comparison identifies the newer 0563 1625 configuration; verify the exact part number and US package.']], [j.service,j.airflow,j.commission],psychrometers,[['JL3RH fits a connected diagnosis','The flexible probe and supported readings are reasons to compare it for air-condition measurements. Its connected range does not tell you where the probe should be placed or establish total airflow.'],['Choose the screen arrangement','An app probe can fit an existing kit, while a local-display instrument can reduce phone dependence. Both still need appropriate placement and current calibration and maintenance practices.'],['Avoid diagnosing from one number','Record conditions with other relevant measurements and the equipment requirements. A single temperature split, humidity percentage or derived wet-bulb reading is not a complete performance diagnosis.']]),
  group('best-anemometers-for-hvac','Best Anemometers for HVAC: Duct Probes and Outlet Measurements','Electrical & Airside','Compare STA2 and 405i for hot-wire duct access, 410i for a connected small vane, the newer 416 for a telescopic 16 mm vane, or 417 for a larger outlet-oriented vane.','These are velocity instruments and calculated-flow arrangements, not substitutes for every capture-hood or balancing task.',[['Choose by access geometry','A narrow duct probe and a large vane at a grille do not take the same measurement. Match the sensor and available approach.'],['Use representative measurements','A single point can misrepresent a non-uniform flow. Follow the instrument method and enter dimensions correctly for a calculated volume.'],['Match accessory and generation','Funnel accessories, telescopes and current app-connected generations change the setup. Compare the complete part number.']],[['Is one register reading total system airflow?','No. Measurement geometry, method and system layout matter.'],['Do I need a capture hood instead?','Some tasks require one. Select the measurement method and equipment required for the actual commissioning or balancing scope.']], [j.airflow,j.commission],airflow,[['STA2 provides a local duct-probe arrangement','Its hot-wire probe and local display make it a useful first comparison for suitable duct measurements. We have not independently compared its accuracy or usability in different duct geometries.'],['Vane size changes the application','The 416’s small telescopic vane and the 417’s larger outlet-oriented vane serve different access conditions. Choose the location and method first, then the instrument.'],['Calculated volume needs sound inputs','An app can calculate a result using entered dimensions, but it cannot make an unrepresentative velocity reading representative. Keep the measurement location, geometry and method with the record.']]),
  group('best-temperature-tools-for-hvac','Best Temperature Tools for HVAC: Contact Probes and Infrared Spot Checks','Electrical & Airside','Start with ST4 for two contact-temperature channels, choose SPK1 or SPK2 for a pocket probe, SPK3 for combined rod and IR readings, or SIG1 for a separate infrared tool.','Contact and infrared measurements answer different questions. None of these is a thermal camera or combustion analyzer.',[['Choose the measurement method','Contact probes and infrared surface readings are not interchangeable. Match the sensor to air, pipe or surface requirements.'],['Check the actual probes','A meter accepting K-type inputs still needs suitable probes for the work. Confirm included bead probes versus separately required pipe clamps.'],['Understand infrared limits','Emissivity, reflections and the spot’s size affect surface readings. Infrared tools do not measure through panels.']],[['Can an IR thermometer measure air temperature?','It measures radiation from surfaces in its field of view, not a direct air-temperature reading.'],['Does ST4 include every application probe?','No. Confirm the supplied probes and budget for suitable accessories.']], [j.service,j.apprentice,j.commission],temperatures,[['ST4 is a dedicated two-channel comparison','It provides contact-temperature channels without requiring a refrigerant manifold. That can suit diagnostic work where temperature difference is the relevant question and the probes are correctly chosen.'],['Pocket tools have a narrower purpose','A small folding instrument can earn daily bag space for supported spot readings. It does not become a full paired measurement kit just because it is convenient.'],['Treat infrared as a surface indication','SPK3 and SIG1 serve documented surface-measurement roles. Compare spot geometry and surface properties before drawing conclusions, and confirm with another appropriate method when the question requires it.']]),
  group('best-valve-core-removal-tools-for-hvac','Best Valve Core Removal Tools for HVAC: Port Size and Gauge Isolation','Evacuation & Recovery','Compare VC1G for visible core engagement, VC1 for a simpler arrangement, VC2G for its second valve, or Appion MGAVCT and MGAVCR for their different service-port sizes.','These are components in a trained refrigerant-handling workflow. Port, pressure and refrigerant compatibility require exact-model checks.',[['Start with the service fitting','1/4-inch and 5/16-inch fittings are different. An adapter or similarly shaped tool is not automatic compatibility.'],['Choose the valve arrangement','Sight glasses and additional valves change handling and gauge isolation. Follow the instructions for the specific tool.'],['Keep the complete setup suitable','Core tools, gauges, hoses and equipment each have their own ratings and handling requirements.']],[['Are MGAVCT and MGAVCR interchangeable?','No. They are listed for different service fitting sizes.'],['Does a sight glass guarantee safe core removal?','No. It is an observation feature within the documented procedure.']], [j.evacuation,j.recovery,j.commission],cores,[['VC1G adds an observation feature','The sight glass is a meaningful difference when observing core engagement is useful. It does not eliminate the need for correct fit, a suitable refrigerant setup and the tool’s handling instructions.'],['Avoid buying by appearance','The Appion alternatives have similar bodies but different fittings. Fieldpiece offers separate port fittings and valve arrangements. Check the actual service connection before comparing the price of a tool.'],['Choose components as a system','A core tool is one connection in a larger evacuation, recovery or charging arrangement. Plan the gauge isolation and hose configuration using the manuals for each component.']]),
  group('best-tubing-cutters-for-hvac','Best Tubing Cutters for HVAC: Tight Access and Copper Preparation','Installation & Carry','Compare RIDGID 101 and 118 for close access, RIDGID 151 or KNIPEX TubiX for a larger adjustable cutter, or Milwaukee 2471-20 for its documented powered copper application.','These are cutting comparisons, not a complete refrigerant-tube preparation kit. Nominal plumbing sizes and actual outside diameter can differ.',[['Use actual outside diameter','Match the cutter capacity and wheel to the material and actual tube OD. Do not assume a nominal pipe size equals the refrigerant tube measurement.'],['Check rotation clearance','A larger cutter or powered tool may not fit even when the tube is within its capacity.'],['Plan the preparation that follows','The required deburring, cleanliness, joining method and inspection depend on the installed system. A cutter is not the whole process.']],[['Will a powered plumbing cutter fit every refrigerant tube?','No. Check its documented material and exact size support, including nominal-size versus OD distinctions.'],['Is cutting the whole preparation process?','No. Follow the installed system’s preparation, joining and verification requirements.']], [j.commission,j.van,j.apprentice],plumbingTools('best-tubing-cutters-for-plumbers'),[['Close-quarters tools solve an access problem','RIDGID 101 is a compact alternative when available rotation space is limited. Larger quick-adjust cutters may be easier on an open installation but need their own working clearance.'],['Keep the size convention visible','Refrigeration work commonly specifies tube outside diameter. A powered cutter marketed by nominal plumbing sizes needs careful checking before it is treated as a refrigeration-tube purchase.'],['Keep clean preparation equipment separate','Choose the joining and preparation method required for the actual system. Tool storage, clean handling and appropriate inspection matter alongside selecting a cutter.']]),
  group('best-tool-bags-for-hvac','Best Tool Bags for HVAC: Daily Diagnosis and Van Organization','Installation & Carry','Choose TP-XL for a compact daily kit, TECH-MCT for a structured enclosed bag, Klein’s smaller tote or CLC 1539 for other pocket arrangements, or PACKOUT 15-inch for an existing modular setup.','A carrying plan is not a recommendation to buy every bag. Keep bulky refrigerant machines and contamination-prone equipment separately organized.',[['Choose the daily load first','Fit the actual meter, leads, hand tools and lighting before choosing a bag by pocket count.'],['Protect measuring equipment','Cases, lead storage and contamination separation matter more than carrying every tool in one open compartment.'],['Plan the van separately','Recovery machines, pumps, hoses and cylinders need their own suitable transport and storage arrangements.']],[['Should pumps and all instruments go in one bag?','Usually they need different carrying and protection arrangements. Choose a daily diagnosis bag and suitable separate machine storage.'],['Is PACKOUT necessary?','No. It is useful only if that modular transport arrangement fits an existing setup.']], [j.van,j.apprentice,j.service],plumbingTools('best-tool-bags-for-plumbers'),[['TP-XL keeps the daily selection deliberate','A compact pouch encourages choosing the tools used on a typical call. That can reduce repeated searching, but it should not force a meter or fragile probe into unsuitable protection.'],['Separate instruments from dirty equipment','Keep sensors, leads and measurement accessories protected. Hoses, machines and workpieces can bring oil or debris that does not belong against delicate instruments.'],['Choose a system that fits the route','A tote, enclosed bag and modular van system each make different carrying tradeoffs. Compare the actual load, access and transport method before buying for branding or pocket count.']]),
];

